---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { guideSection, beyondPsySection } from "../../sidebars";

const params = [
  {
    name: "minw",
    default: "(0.01 + 1.8/√T)·T",
    body: "The minimum window size, in observations. Too short and the ADF regression inside it has no power; too long and early, short-lived bubbles fall below the floor and can't be tested at all. The default is Phillips, Shi & Yu's rule of thumb, a function of the sample size T alone — pass an integer to override it.",
  },
  {
    name: "lag",
    default: "0",
    body: "The number of lagged Δy terms in the ADF regression — the same augmentation as ordinary ADF, applied inside every recursive window. Fixed, 0 by default. The precomputed critical values cover lag 0 to 4, so a lagged run needs no simulation of its own; for the sieve bootstrap, radf_sb_cv(type = \"aic\" / \"bic\") can pick the lag automatically, once per series.",
  },
];

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "parameters", text: "minw and lag" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "which-critical-values", text: "Which critical values to read against" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "monte-carlo", text: "Monte Carlo" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "wild-bootstrap", text: "Wild bootstrap" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "other-bootstraps", text: "Other bootstraps" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "shared-store", text: "Shared store" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "panel-statistics", text: "Panel statistics" },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  eyebrow="Guide · 3 of 3"
  title="Settings and critical values"
  description="radf() takes only two parameters, window size and lag length. Everything else about a test's sensitivity comes from the critical values you read the statistic against, and GSADF has no closed-form distribution: every threshold is simulated, either once for a lookup table or from your own data."
  headings={headings}
  sidebar={[guideSection(), await beyondPsySection()]}
>
  <section id="parameters" class="scroll-mt-24 flex flex-col gap-10">
    <div>
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">Settings</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl text-ink">Two parameters: minw and lag.</h2>
    </div>
    {params.map((p) => (
      <article class="grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-[140px_1fr] sm:gap-6">
        <div>
          <code class="font-mono text-base text-ink">{p.name}</code>
          <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">default: {p.default}</p>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{p.body}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
      Both are arguments to <a href="/reference/radf" class="text-ink underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-explosive"><code class="text-ink">radf()</code></a> —
      see its full reference entry for the return value and worked examples.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="which-critical-values" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-border pt-14 mt-14">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">The other choice</p>
    <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl text-ink">Which critical values to read against.</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
      The statistic from <code class="text-ink">radf()</code> means nothing without a threshold.
      Three ways to get one, in increasing order of how much they cost to compute — and of how
      well they match your actual data:
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-border bg-border sm:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 bg-panel p-6">
        <span class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">Precomputed (default)</span>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">Monte Carlo tables simulated once for every T up to 4,000 and lag 0–4, fetched on first use and cached. What you get when you pass no <code class="text-ink">cv</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 bg-panel p-6">
        <span class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">Monte Carlo</span>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">Simulated under the null (a random walk) for your exact T and <code class="text-ink">minw</code>. Assumes homoskedastic Gaussian innovations. See <a href="/reference/radf_mc_cv" class="text-ink underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-explosive"><code class="text-ink">radf_mc_cv()</code></a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 bg-panel p-6">
        <span class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">Bootstrap</span>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">Built from your own data, so it inherits the heteroskedasticity or autocorrelation actually there: wild (<a href="/reference/radf_wb_cv" class="text-ink underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-explosive"><code class="text-ink">radf_wb_cv()</code></a>, <a href="/reference/radf_wb_ps_cv" class="text-ink underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-explosive"><code class="text-ink">radf_wb_ps_cv()</code></a>) or sieve (<a href="/reference/radf_sb_cv" class="text-ink underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-explosive"><code class="text-ink">radf_sb_cv()</code></a>, panel).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="monte-carlo" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-border pt-14 mt-14">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">Monte Carlo</p>
    <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl text-ink">Simulate the null directly.</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
      Draw a pure random walk — <code class="text-ink">cumsum(rnorm(n))</code>, nothing else — run
      GSADF on it, and repeat. A few thousand replications later, the 90th/95th/99th percentile
      of that empirical distribution is the critical value at that sample size. Exact for that T
      and <code class="text-ink">minw</code>, but assumes homoskedastic Gaussian innovations,
      which real series rarely have. See <a href="/reference/radf_mc_cv" class="text-ink underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-explosive"><code class="text-ink">radf_mc_cv()</code></a>.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
      The default tables were built exactly this way: 2,000 seeded replications for every
      lag from 0 to 4 and every sample size up to 4,000 — then served as a lookup so a typical
      <code class="text-ink">radf()</code> call doesn't re-simulate anything (see the shared store below).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="wild-bootstrap" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-border pt-14 mt-14">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">Wild bootstrap</p>
    <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl text-ink">Simulate the null from your data.</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
      Instead of drawing fresh Gaussian noise, fit the null model to the actual series, take its
      residuals, and resample them with random ± sign weights each replication. Whatever
      heteroskedasticity is really in the data — volatility clustering, a variance break, fat
      tails — survives into the simulated critical value instead of being assumed away.
      Correspondingly more expensive: it's specific to one dataset, not reusable across series.
      See <a href="/reference/radf_wb_cv" class="text-ink underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-explosive"><code class="text-ink">radf_wb_cv()</code></a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="other-bootstraps" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-border pt-14 mt-14">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">Variants</p>
    <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl text-ink">Other bootstraps, for other problems.</h2>
    <ul class="mt-6 max-w-xl space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
      <li><strong class="font-medium text-ink">Skewed innovations.</strong> <code class="text-ink">radf_wb_cv(dist_skew = TRUE)</code> draws Hafner's (2020) right-skewed multiplier weights, for series whose returns are themselves strongly right-skewed — cryptocurrencies, in the paper's own application.</li>
      <li><strong class="font-medium text-ink">Phillips–Shi wild bootstrap.</strong> Fits a null autoregression (lag fixed or chosen by AIC/BIC), resamples its residuals, and simulates a sample of chosen length — robust to non-stationary volatility, and the bootstrap real-time <code class="text-ink">monitor()</code> uses for its training window. See <a href="/reference/radf_wb_ps_cv" class="text-ink underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-explosive"><code class="text-ink">radf_wb_ps_cv()</code></a>.</li>
      <li><strong class="font-medium text-ink">Sieve bootstrap.</strong> Fits an autoregression to each series and resamples its residuals, preserving serial correlation; gives critical values for the panel statistic. Lag fixed or chosen by AIC/BIC (Pedersen &amp; Schütte, 2020). See <a href="/reference/radf_sb_cv" class="text-ink underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-explosive"><code class="text-ink">radf_sb_cv()</code></a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="shared-store" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-border pt-14 mt-14">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">Shared store</p>
    <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl text-ink">One table, served, not bundled.</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
      <code class="text-ink">exuber</code> and <code class="text-ink">pyexuber</code>
      read the same precomputed tables from a small read-only store: one object per
      (<code class="text-ink">lag</code>, <code class="text-ink">n</code>), simulated once with
      2,000 replications, fetched on first use and cached on disk. In R this is automatic —
      any <code class="text-ink">summary()</code>, <code class="text-ink">datestamp()</code> or
      <code class="text-ink">autoplot()</code> without an explicit <code class="text-ink">cv</code> uses it. In Python it's
      <code class="text-ink">exuber.radf_crit(n, lag)</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
      Coverage: <code class="text-ink">lag</code> 0 through 4, every sample size from the smallest
      the PSY window allows up to <code class="text-ink">n = 4,000</code>. Beyond that,
      <code class="text-ink">radf_mc_cv()</code> / <code class="text-ink">radf_wb_cv()</code>
      remain the way to compute your own.
    </p>
    <span class="mt-6 inline-block rounded-full border border-border px-3 py-1 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
      shared critical-value store — live
    </span>
  </section>

  <section id="panel-statistics" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-border pt-14 mt-14">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">Multiple series</p>
    <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl text-ink">Panel statistics come free.</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
      There's no separate panel setting. Pass <code class="text-ink">radf()</code> a matrix or
      data frame with more than one column and it computes the univariate statistics for every
      series in one pass, plus <code class="text-ink">bsadf_panel</code> and
      <code class="text-ink">gsadf_panel</code> — the cross-sectional average and its supremum —
      as a by-product, at close to no extra cost. The panel statistic is tested against the
      sieve bootstrap's panel critical values (<a href="/reference/radf_sb_cv" class="text-ink underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-explosive"><code class="text-ink">radf_sb_cv()</code></a>).
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
